<template>
    <heading>Browser database</heading>
    <keywords><li>IndexedDB</li><li>browserDB</li><li>persistence</li><li>key value</li></keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p>Browser preferences and remembered selections belong to a browser profile and application origin, whereas project articles must travel with the shared project. <code>window.LP5.util.browserDB</code> provides the former without imposing project-specific record types or key allocation. Its Promise-returning interface is <code>load(key)</code> (value or undefined), <code>store(key, value)</code> (create or replace), <code>remove(key)</code> (absent is harmless), and <code>entries()</code> (an array of [key, value] pairs in IndexedDB key order). Store and remove return no value. Callers normally use descriptive string keys; native IndexedDB key rules apply.</p>
        <p>Values use IndexedDB structured cloning, not JSON. Objects, arrays, binary values, and supported browser objects such as directory handles can be stored directly. Functions and DOM nodes cannot. Loaded values are copies; editing them does not persist changes until store is called. A stored undefined value and a missing key both load as undefined; entries distinguishes them.</p>
        <p>The database is <code>LP5-browserDB</code>, version 1, with a single <code>values</code> object store. Connections are kept only for an operation so idle callers do not hold them open across schema upgrades. This database is separate from the existing directory-handle utility; no migration or current-caller changes are performed. Initialization is lazy, so simply loading Utilities performs no database I/O.</p>
        <p>Operations reject on failure; successful mutations resolve only after transaction completion. Await a store before depending on its result in a later load. Separate calls do not form a shared transaction, and load/edit/store is not an atomic update across tabs. A blocked database upgrade rejects with an explanatory error; a late successful connection is closed. Version-change notifications close open connections.</p>
        <p>Browser storage can be cleared, so remembered selections cannot be the only copy of shared project data. Project-key allocation, URL normalization, handle identity comparisons using isSameEntry, and permission checks belong to callers; this utility contains no project-specific policy.</p>
    </section>
    <section data-lp5-kind="code">
        <name>Browser database</name>
        <code><![CDATA[const browserDB = (() => {
    function open() {
        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            const request = window.indexedDB.open("LP5-browserDB", 1);
            let blocked = false;
            request.onupgradeneeded = () => {
                request.result.createObjectStore("values");
            };
            request.onblocked = () => {
                blocked = true;
                reject(new Error("Browser database upgrade is blocked. Close other LP5 tabs and reload the page."));
            };
            request.onerror = () => reject(request.error);
            request.onsuccess = () => {
                const database = request.result;
                database.onversionchange = () => database.close();
                if (blocked) database.close();
                else resolve(database);
            };
        });
    }

    async function transact(mode, operation) {
        const database = await open();
        try {
            return await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
                const transaction = database.transaction("values", mode);
                let result;
                transaction.oncomplete = () => resolve(result());
                transaction.onabort = () => reject(
                    transaction.error || new Error("Browser database transaction aborted")
                );
                try {
                    result = operation(transaction.objectStore("values"));
                } catch (error) {
                    transaction.abort();
                    reject(error);
                }
            });
        } finally {
            database.close();
        }
    }

    function load(key) {
        return transact("readonly", values => {
            const request = values.get(key);
            return () => request.result;
        });
    }

    function store(key, value) {
        return transact("readwrite", values => {
            values.put(value, key);
            return () => undefined;
        });
    }

    function remove(key) {
        return transact("readwrite", values => {
            values.delete(key);
            return () => undefined;
        });
    }

    function entries() {
        return transact("readonly", values => {
            const keys = values.getAllKeys();
            const records = values.getAll();
            return () => keys.result.map((key, index) => [key, records.result[index]]);
        });
    }

    return { load, store, remove, entries };
})();
]]></code>
    </section>
</template>
